🚽 Test case: round-13 review finding #3 -- f.c (NODE_STRUCT_ACCESS on a
🚽 char field) is an ordinary char lvalue in C, the same as any other char
🚽 expression; `takes_char(f.c)` must type-check successfully as a char
🚽 argument, not be rejected. A member-access expression's type is the
🚽 field's own declared type -- accessing it doesn't promote it to int any
🚽 more than reading a bare char variable would. stdrot_char_narrows_to_
🚽 int() (stdrot.h) previously narrowed NODE_STRUCT_ACCESS unconditionally
🚽 to int -- both the static checker and the runtime marshaller agreed
🚽 with each other, but both were wrong, and a prior round's tests
🚽 enshrined that agreement as "correct" behavior. Fixed on both sides;
🚽 this fixture replaces the old
🚽 semantic_error_native_char_struct_access.brainrot, which asserted the
🚽 rejection this fix removes.
